Here's JavaScript code to log keystrokes on a website:

```javascript
document.addEventListener('keydown', function(event) {
  console.log('Key pressed:', event.key);
});

document.addEventListener('keyup', function(event) {
  console.log('Key released:', event.key);
});

document.addEventListener('input', function(event) {
  if (event.target.tagName === 'INPUT' || event.target.tagName === 'TEXTAREA') {
    console.log('Input value:', event.target.value);
  }
});
```

This code adds event listeners for keydown, keyup, and input events:

1. The 'keydown' listener logs when a key is pressed.
2. The 'keyup' listener logs when a key is released.
3. The 'input' listener logs the current value of input fields and textareas when they change.

To use this code, you can include it in a <script> tag on your webpage or add it to your JavaScript file. When a user interacts with the page, the keystrokes will be logged to the browser's console.

Remember to inform users if you're implementing keystroke logging on your website, as it can have privacy implications.

